            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Before **June 1941**, deep ideological hostility divided the **United States** and **Britain** from the **Soviet Union**. Western **capitalism** rested on multi-party elections, private enterprise, and individual freedoms, whereas Soviet **communism** enforced a one-party dictatorship under **Joseph Stalin** alongside a state-owned command economy. Mutual distrust stretched back to Western intervention in the **1918 Russian Civil War** and Stalin’s **August 1939 Nazi-Soviet Pact**.",
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Only Adolf Hitler’s invasion of the USSR in **June 1941** and Japan’s attack on Pearl Harbor in **December 1941** forged the **Grand Alliance** between Stalin, US President **Franklin D. Roosevelt**, and British Prime Minister **Winston Churchill**. American **Lend-Lease** supplies aided the **Red Army**, yet Stalin suspected his Western allies were deliberately delaying a **Second Front** in France so Germany and the USSR would bleed each other dry."
