          "title": "Atomic Diplomacy & The End of Trust",
          "theme_heading": "Atomic Diplomacy & The End of Trust",
          "act_title": "Stage 5: Atomic Diplomacy & The End of Trust — How did the US atomic bomb transform superpower relations in 1945?",
          "paragraphs": [
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> On **16 July 1945**, the day before **Potsdam** opened, American scientists successfully tested the world’s first **atomic bomb** in New Mexico. Emboldened that Washington no longer needed **Red Army** help to defeat Japan, **Truman** casually informed **Stalin** on **24 July** that the USA possessed a weapon of *\"unusual destructive force\"*—unaware that Soviet spies inside the **Manhattan Project** had already briefed Stalin.",
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> When the United States dropped atomic bombs on **Hiroshima** (**6 August 1945**) and **Nagasaki** (**9 August 1945**), forcing Japan’s surrender without Soviet occupation of the Japanese mainland, Stalin saw the bombings as **atomic intimidation** aimed at Moscow. Rather than making Stalin compliant, the US nuclear monopoly drove him to tighten his buffer zone across **Eastern Europe** and order Soviet physicists under **Lavrentiy Beria** to build a rival Soviet atomic bomb, achieved in **August 1949**."
          ],
