          "act_title": "Stage 1: Rákosi’s Terror & Khrushchev’s \"Secret Speech\" — Why did discontent explode across Hungary in October 1956?",
          "paragraphs": [
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Since **1949**, Hungary had suffered brutal Stalinist rule under hardline Communist dictator **Mátyás Rákosi**, who styled himself *\"Stalin’s best pupil\"*. Rákosi’s feared secret police, the **ÁVH**, arrested over **380,000** citizens and executed thousands, banned Catholic religious education (imprisoning Cardinal Mindszenty), censored the press, and forced Hungarian industry under **Comecon** to ship cheap coal and machinery to the **Soviet Union** while living standards fell.",
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Following **Stalin**’s death in **March 1953**, new Soviet leader **Nikita Khrushchev** raised hopes of reform across Eastern Europe. In his **\"Secret Speech\"** of **February 1956**, Khrushchev denounced Stalin’s purges and promised **\"peaceful coexistence\"** and **de-Stalinisation**. Inspired by Polish worker protests in June 1956, students in **Budapest** marched on **23 October 1956** demanding free elections, press freedom, and the withdrawal of Soviet troops, toppling a giant bronze statue of Stalin."
          ],
