          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> Hoping to calm the **Budapest** street fighting, **Khrushchev** appointed popular reformist Communist **Imre Nagy** as Hungarian Prime Minister on **24 October 1956** and pulled Red Army tanks out of the capital. Within days, however, Nagy went far beyond the limited domestic reforms Moscow had tolerated in Poland: he dissolved the hated **ÁVH**, released political prisoners, ended one-party Communist rule, and invited non-communist parties into a coalition government promising **free elections**.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Then, on **1 November 1956**, Nagy took the fateful step of announcing that **Hungary** would withdraw completely from the **Warsaw Pact**, declare Cold War neutrality like Austria, and appeal to the **United Nations** to protect Hungarian independence against Soviet interference."
