          "act_title": "Stage 5: Western Inaction, Suez & Cold War Consequences — Why did the USA and UN take no military action to save Hungary in 1956?",
          "paragraphs": [
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> Many Hungarians expected Western military help because US-funded **Radio Free Europe** broadcasts and Secretary of State John Foster Dulles had talked of **\"rolling back\"** communism. Yet **President Dwight D. Eisenhower** refused to send troops: both superpowers now possessed **hydrogen bombs**, and intervening inside an existing **Warsaw Pact** state risked nuclear World War Three. Furthermore, Britain and France were simultaneously invading Egypt during the **October–November 1956 Suez Crisis**, dividing NATO and blunting Western moral condemnation.",
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> International reaction was therefore limited to **United Nations** resolutions condemning the invasion, **$20 million** in US humanitarian relief, resettling **200,000 Hungarian refugees**, and a handful of Western boycotts of the **1956 Melbourne Olympics**. While Khrushchev tightened his grip over Eastern Europe, \"peaceful coexistence\" rang hollow and thousands of Western European Communists resigned in disgust—setting up a sharper superpower clash over **Berlin** in **1958**."
          ],
