          "title": "Source A: Nikita Khrushchev’s Berlin Ultimatum Note to the Western Powers (27 November 1958)",
          "text": "The Western powers have turned West Berlin into a kind of state within a state, using it as a springboard for intensive espionage, sabotage, and provocative activity against the German Democratic Republic and the Soviet Union... The most correct and natural way to solve the problem would be for the western part of Berlin to be reunited with its eastern part. Yet wishing to avoid painful disruption, the Soviet Government proposes making West Berlin an independent political unit—a demilitarised free city, with no foreign troops stationed there—within six months.",
          "content": "The Western powers have turned West Berlin into a kind of state within a state, using it as a springboard for intensive espionage, sabotage, and provocative activity against the German Democratic Republic and the Soviet Union... The most correct and natural way to solve the problem would be for the western part of Berlin to be reunited with its eastern part. Yet wishing to avoid painful disruption, the Soviet Government proposes making West Berlin an independent political unit—a demilitarised free city, with no foreign troops stationed there—within six months.",
