            "examiner_tip": "Edexcel caps answers at 4/8 if you only describe the 1958 ultimatum and the 1961 Wall! Bridge the gap with own knowledge on the 2.7m–3m refugee \"brain drain\", the May 1960 Paris/U-2 collapse, and the June 1961 Vienna Summit.",
            "model": "Between 1949 and 1958, nearly three million East Germans—including vital doctors, engineers, and teachers—fled low wages and Stasi repression under Walter Ulbricht through West Berlin’s open border. Determined to halt this crippling \"brain drain\", Nikita Khrushchev issued his Berlin ultimatum on 27 November 1958, demanding Western troops leave West Berlin within six months.<br><br>Because neither superpower risked nuclear war, the ultimatum triggered a series of summit meetings between 1959 and 1961. Although Khrushchev withdrew his deadline after meeting Eisenhower at Camp David (September 1959), Soviet downing of Gary Powers’s U-2 spy plane wrecked the May 1960 Paris Summit. At the June 1961 Vienna Summit, Khrushchev renewed his six-month ultimatum to test young President John F. Kennedy; however, Kennedy refused to yield, calling up 250,000 reservists and adding $3.2 billion to US defence spending, which sparked a surge of over 30,000 East German refugees in July 1961 alone.<br><br>Realising Kennedy would fight to defend West Berlin—yet would not invade East Berlin—Khrushchev backed Ulbricht’s request to seal the border. Before dawn on 13 August 1961, East German troops erected barbed wire and then a 3.6-metre concrete Berlin Wall, halting the refugee exodus and ending the ultimatum crisis despite October’s tense Checkpoint Charlie tank standoff.",
