            "question": "3. Explain the importance of the construction of the Berlin Wall (1961) for US-Soviet relations. [8 marks]",
            "prompt": "Write two distinct analytical paragraphs: (1) How sealing the border reduced the immediate danger of nuclear war over Berlin (ending the 1958 ultimatum), and (2) How the Wall reshaped propaganda rivalry (Kennedy’s June 1963 \"Ich bin ein Berliner\" visit).",
            "examiner_tip": "Capture the Cold War paradox: the Wall stabilised superpower military tensions over Berlin while handing the USA a major ideological propaganda victory.",
            "model": "First, the construction of the Berlin Wall from 13 August 1961 was important for US-Soviet relations because it paradoxically reduced the immediate risk of nuclear war over Germany. By physically halting the exodus of nearly three million East German refugees, the Wall stabilised Walter Ulbricht’s GDR economy and allowed Nikita Khrushchev quietly to drop his 1958 ultimatum demanding Western withdrawal from West Berlin. Even after the tense 18-hour tank standoff at Checkpoint Charlie in October 1961 ended peacefully, President Kennedy privately concluded that \"a wall is a hell of a lot better than a war\" because Allied garrison rights in West Berlin remained intact.<br><br>Second, the Wall was important because it handed the United States a powerful propaganda weapon in the global ideological struggle while cementing Europe’s division until 1989. Because Communist authorities shot citizens attempting to flee—such as 18-year-old Peter Fechter in August 1962—the concrete barrier proved worldwide that communism had to imprison its own people. Visiting West Berlin in June 1963, Kennedy proclaimed \"Ich bin ein Berliner\" before 120,000 citizens, turning the Wall into an enduring symbol of Western freedom versus Soviet repression.",
