          "act_title": "Stage 4: The Brezhnev Doctrine & One-Party Rule — What caused Leonid Brezhnev to announce the Brezhnev Doctrine in 1968?",
          "image": "/images/gcse_cold_war/kt2/cw_l8_b3_leonid_brezhnev_sed_congress_1971.jpg",
          "image_alt": "Black-and-white Bundesarchiv photograph of Soviet General Secretary Leonid Brezhnev clasping hands with East German First Secretary Erich Honecker on the rostrum of the 8th SED Party Congress beneath a giant portrait of Vladimir Lenin",
          "caption": "Soviet General Secretary Leonid Brezhnev clasping hands with East German leader Erich Honecker beneath a giant portrait of Lenin at the SED Party Congress, symbolising the Brezhnev Doctrine’s enforcement of one-party bloc loyalty.",
          "isReversed": true,
          "paragraphs": [
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Because Czechoslovakia had never threatened to leave the **Warsaw Pact**, **Leonid Brezhnev** needed a sweeping ideological justification for invading a fellow socialist ally. First outlined in the Soviet party newspaper ***Pravda*** on **26 September 1968** and proclaimed formally by Brezhnev in Warsaw in **November 1968**, this policy became known in the West as the **Brezhnev Doctrine**.",
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Brezhnev declared that once a country had become socialist, communism was irreversible and its sovereignty remained subordinate to the survival of the wider Eastern Bloc. Whenever internal or external forces threatened **one-party Communist rule** or risked detaching any state from the **Warsaw Pact**, every Communist state had a collective right and duty to intervene militarily—locking Eastern European satellites under Kremlin obedience until **Mikhail Gorbachev** abandoned the doctrine in **1989**."
          ],
