          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Because Czechoslovakia had never threatened to leave the **Warsaw Pact**, **Leonid Brezhnev** needed a sweeping ideological justification for invading a fellow socialist ally. First outlined in the Soviet party newspaper ***Pravda*** on **26 September 1968** and proclaimed formally by Brezhnev in Warsaw in **November 1968**, this policy became known in the West as the **Brezhnev Doctrine**.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Brezhnev declared that once a country had become socialist, communism was irreversible and its sovereignty remained subordinate to the survival of the wider Eastern Bloc. Whenever internal or external forces threatened **one-party Communist rule** or risked detaching any state from the **Warsaw Pact**, every Communist state had a collective right and duty to intervene militarily—locking Eastern European satellites under Kremlin obedience until **Mikhail Gorbachev** abandoned the doctrine in **1989**."
