          "question_badge": "Strategic Motivation",
          "question_type": "Strategic Motivation",
          "model_answer": "Because Nixon had impeccable anti-communist credentials, conservative critics could rarely accuse him of being \"soft\" on communism when he used triangular diplomacy with China and SALT 1 with Moscow to ease Vietnam War pressures and curb defence costs.",
          "context": "Photographed during Nixon’s first term as he and Henry Kissinger prepared historic summits in Beijing and Moscow.",
          "source_context": "Drained by $150bn+ Vietnam War costs and domestic inflation, Richard Nixon and Henry Kissinger exploited the Sino-Soviet split by visiting Beijing in February 1972 before flying to Moscow in May 1972.",
          "teacher_notes": {
            "source_context": "Drained by $150bn+ Vietnam War costs and domestic inflation, Richard Nixon and Henry Kissinger exploited the Sino-Soviet split by visiting Beijing in February 1972 before flying to Moscow in May 1972. **Hinge Question:** How did Nixon’s February 1972 visit to Mao’s China give Washington extra leverage over Brezhnev three months later in Moscow?",
            "hinge_question": "How did Nixon’s February 1972 visit to Mao’s China give Washington extra leverage over Brezhnev three months later in Moscow?"
          },
          "question": "Why was a staunch lifelong anti-communist like President Richard Nixon (Source A) politically able—and eager—to pursue Détente with Beijing and Moscow in 1972?"
