            "model": "First, SALT 1 in May 1972 was crucially important for attempts to reduce superpower tensions because it established the first binding nuclear arms limits and unlocked wider East–West diplomacy. By restricting each superpower to just two Anti-Ballistic Missile (ABM) sites and freezing strategic ICBM and SLBM launchers for five years, Nixon and Brezhnev stabilised Mutually Assured Destruction (MAD) and pledged peaceful coexistence under the Basic Principles Agreement. This breakthrough proved Washington and Moscow could negotiate constructively, paving the way for 1973–74 trade summits, major US grain exports, the July 1975 Apollo–Soyuz mission, and the August 1975 Helsinki Accords.<br><br>Second, SALT 1 was also important because its major technical loopholes limited how deeply tensions fell, forcing difficult follow-up negotiations throughout the 1970s. Because the five-year Interim Agreement froze missile launchers rather than warheads and left Multiple Independently Targeted Re-entry Vehicles (MIRVs) completely unrestricted, nuclear warhead stockpiles continued multiplying on both sides. Consequently, underlying strategic distrust persisted and compelled Carter and Brezhnev to spend years negotiating SALT 2 (1979).",
