          "id": "gcse_cw_l12_source_b",
          "letter": "B",
          "title": "Source B: East and West Berliners Celebrating Atop the Berlin Wall at the Brandenburg Gate (November 1989)",
          "image": "/images/cold_war/berlin_wall_fall_1989.jpg",
          "src": "/images/cold_war/berlin_wall_fall_1989.jpg",
          "source": "/images/cold_war/berlin_wall_fall_1989.jpg",
          "image_alt": "Large crowds of East and West Berliners standing shoulder-to-shoulder atop the graffiti-covered Berlin Wall holding banners reading LOVE & PEACE IN WEST & EAST directly before the Brandenburg Gate, November 1989",
          "caption": "East and West Berliners standing atop the Berlin Wall in front of the Brandenburg Gate on 10 November 1989 (Bundesarchiv Bild 183-1989-1110-018) with banners proclaiming \"Love & Peace in West & East\".",
          "provenance": "German Federal Archives (Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-1989-1110-018, photographed 10 November 1989).",
          "citation": "Bundesarchiv, Koblenz (CC BY-SA 3.0 DE).",
          "question_badge": "Strategic Significance",
          "question_type": "Strategic Significance",
          "model_answer": "Symbolically it destroyed the concrete barrier that had epitomised Communist repression since August 1961, while practically opening free movement across Berlin made East Germany’s survival as a separate Communist state impossible—leading to German reunification within eleven months.",
          "context": "Photographed at the Brandenburg Gate the morning after Günter Schabowski’s press conference inadvertently opened the border on 9 November 1989.",
          "source_context": "For twenty-eight years East German border troops had operated under shoot-to-kill orders along the Death Strip, yet on 9–10 November 1989 without Soviet backing or clear orders, guards stepped aside as citizens reclaimed the Brandenburg Gate.",
          "teacher_notes": {
            "source_context": "For twenty-eight years East German border troops had operated under shoot-to-kill orders along the Death Strip, yet on 9–10 November 1989 without Soviet backing or clear orders, guards stepped aside as citizens reclaimed the Brandenburg Gate. **Hinge Question:** Why did East German border guards at Bornholmer Straße choose to open the barriers on the night of 9 November rather than use lethal force?",
            "hinge_question": "Why did East German border guards at Bornholmer Straße choose to open the barriers on the night of 9 November rather than use lethal force?"
          },
          "question": "Why was the scene captured in Source B both a symbolic and a practical turning point for the end of the Cold War in Europe?"
